'''SM-33''', nicknamed "'''Thirty-Three'''," was a [[droid]] [[pirate]]<ref name="Databank" /> who served as [[First Mate]] of the [[starship]] ''[[Onyx Cinder]]''.<ref name="People">{{WebCite|url=https://people.com/jude-law-teases-star-wars-easter-eggs-in-new-skeleton-crew-series-exclusive-8686428|text=Jude Law Teases 'Childhood Adventure' and ''Star Wars'' Easter Eggs in ''Skeleton Crew'' Series &mdash; See the First Look! (Exclusive)|author=Kang, Esther|date=2024-07-31|work=[[Wikipedia:People (magazine)|''People'']]|archivedate=20240731153133}}</ref> At some point the ''Onyx Cinder'' became buried in a [[forest]] on [[At Attin]] and by the early [[New Republic Era]]. In [[9 ABY]],<ref name="9ABY">{{SkeletonCrewDate}}</ref> a [[youngling]] named [[Wim]] discovered the ''Onyx Cinder'' and, together with three other children named [[Neel]], [[Fern]], and [[KB]], investigated the ship and in the process accidentally activated it and traveled past the [[barrier]], jumped into [[hyperspace]], and eventually became stranded in [[Realspace|space]].<ref name="Adventure" /> The [[Skeleton Crew|group of kids]] then forged an alliance with SM-33 and a [[force-sensitive]] man called [[Jod Na Nawood]] to find back home and avoid a [[Silvo's pirates|band of pirates]].<ref name="Barrier" />

==Biography==
===Adventure at At Achrann===
{{Quote|C-Can't say I remember no At Attin.|SM-33 unable to recall At Attin|<ref name="Remember">{{SkeletonCrew|Can't Say I Remember No At Attin}}</ref>}}
Sometime before [[9 ABY]],<ref name="9ABY" /> SM-33, along with [[Captain]] [[Tak Rennod]]<ref name="Lot to Learn" /> of the ''[[Onyx Cinder]]'', visited the [[planet]] [[At Achrann]], one of the nine [[Jewels of the Old Republic]]. On the planet, they sneaked past warriors similar to those of the [[Hattan]] and eventually arrived at a tower, the [[Fallen Sanctum]]. Within the tower were [[Galactic coordinates]] for all nine Jewels of the Old Republic. The [[captain]] [[Death|murdered]] everyone in his crew who saw the [[Galactic coordinates|coordinates]] for the planet [[At Attin]], another Jewel, and ordered SM-33 to destroy the coordinates and forget everything about the planet. He also instructed the droid to kill anyone looking for the mythical treasure of At Attin, specifying to "pull them apart."<ref name="Remember" />

===Stranded at At Attin===
{{Quote|It's just a droid.''"<br />"''A dead droid.|Fern and Wim|<ref name="Adventure" />}}
SM-33 and his captain headed to the coordinates specified in the star chart of the Fallen Sanctum,<ref name="Remember" /> arriving to the famed At Attin. However, the captain was betrayed and killed, and the ''Onyx Cinder'' crashed upside down. After the crash, SM-33 became deactivated and was eventually overgrown with [[Plant|roots]]. His empty left eye socket also became home<ref name="Adventure" />  to a [[bilge rat]], [[Snowball]], who damaged SM-33's [[memory circuit]]s.<ref name="Snowball">{{Databank|snowball|Snowball}}</ref>

In 9 ABY,<ref name="9ABY" /> four [[Youngling|children]]&mdash;[[Fern]], [[KB]], [[Neel]], and [[Wim]]&mdash;discovered and entered the ''Onyx Cinder'', believing it to be an ancient [[Jedi]] temple. While exploring the [[starship]], Neel mistook the shut-off droid for a [[skeleton]]. As the other children investigated, they determined him to be a deactivated droid who seemingly died trying to pull a lever. As Fern and Wim bickered, Neel investigated SM-33's empty socket, believing there to be something inside. As Snowball emerged, a panicked Neel accidentally jostled SM-33's [[Limb|hand]], which pulled the lever and closed the ship's hatch. After investigating the ship further, the children restored power to the ''Onyx Cinder'' and accidentally triggered the [[autopilot]]. As the children fled to the now unlocked hatch, they passed by SM-33, who, unbeknownst to them, reactivated. Failing to exit the ship before it left At Attin, the children returned to the cockpit and discussed their situation.<ref name="Adventure" />

The children's conversation was interrupted by SM-33 clanking around the hull and letting out an echoing snarl as he made his way to the cockpit. He scared the children as he emerged from behind a mist veil. Believing them to be intruders, SM-33 confronted them and dragged Wim away, only to be stopped by Fern who claimed that she killed the captain and assume command of the ship herself. Recognizing her as the rightful new owner of the ''Cinder'', SM-33 swore allegiance to her.<ref name="Barrier" />

===Serving a new captain===
As Wim questioned her authority, the droid suggested they duel to the death, which put an end to the fighting. He then gave the kids a tour of the ship, and recognizing the need for immediate repairs due to the years of accumulated damage, he suggested they jump to the [[shadowport]] [[Borgo Prime]]. However, the kids asked to be taken back to At Attin instead, activating SM-33's order from his original captain which prevented him from revealing his memories of the planet. Without options, Fern reluctantly agreed to head to Port Borgo.<ref name="Barrier" />

Upon arriving at Borgo, the kids got separated and Wim accidentally revealed his Old Republic credits to a bartender, prompting the pirates to attack them to steal the rest. SM-33 came to their rescue, defending them against the pirates and incapacitating several of them before being shot in the head by [[Brutus]]. The kids were then taken to the brig, while SM-33 was moved to the droid bay.<ref name="Barrier" />
[[File:SM33-TheRealGoodGuys.png|thumb|291x291px|left|A decapitated SM-33.]]
While in the brig, the kids met [[Jod Na Nawood]], a [[Force-sensitive]] individual<ref name="Barrier" /> who helped them escape in exchange for passage off-world. The kids demanded he rescue SM-33 as well before returning to their ship, and Nawood complied, using his connections with the droidmaster [[Benjar Pranic]] to liberate SM-33. Despite being pursued by pirates, the crew reached the ship and escaped Borgo before reactivating SM-33. The droid again attempted to defend the ship against the perceived intruder, but the kids assured him Nawood was a friend. The crew then took the ''Onyx Cinder'' to the [[Observatory Moon]] where they hoped Nawood's [[astronomer]] contact [[Kh'ymm]] would be able to direct them to At Attin.<ref name="Very">{{SkeletonCrew|Very Interesting, As an Astrogation Problem}}</ref>

==Characteristics==
[[File:SM-33-LinkedIn.png|thumb|319x319px|SM-33 preparing for combat with his shields.]]
{{Quote|We are not pirates.''"<br />"''You're not pirates?''"<br />"''We're kids.''"<br />"''Why cannot kids&mdash;|Fern and SM-33|<ref name="Barrier" />}}
SM-33 had a cranky demeanor.<ref name="People" /> Though at times bound by strict programming and the laws of the [[Pirate's Code]], SM-33 would ultimately show personal favor for the [[Skeleton Crew]], and was capable of bending said laws for their sake if the orders were '[[Claimsies|close enough]]' to the Pirate's Code.<ref name="Twitter">{{Twitter|@ForceGhostPod|status/1822745758170566803|Force Ghost Conversations|date=2024-08-11|quote=Details on SM-33 from #SkeletonCrew #D23|archivedate=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/yVJjj}}</ref> He was extremely loyal to [[Tak Rennod]], the former captain of the ''Onyx Cinder''.<ref name="Databank" /> SM-33 was skilled in direct [[Unarmed combat|hand-to-hand combat]], and would primarily utilize retractable physical shields built into his forearms. Though durable enough to withstand conventional blaster fire, SM-33 was particularly susceptible to [[Ion blaster|ion weapons]] and [[restraining bolt]]s. SM-33 was missing his left [[photoreceptor]], as well as his left foot.<ref name="Barrier" /> The droid had a grilled [[vocabulator]] and a [[rubber]]ized accordion neck.<ref name="AdventureAD">{{SkeletonCrew|This Could Be a Real Adventure}} {{AD|Disney+}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|Jon pitched it as, 'Kids lost in the ''Star Wars'' universe. And there's a bunch of pirates.' I was like, 'Cool. I just finished a ''Peter Pan'' movie, I'm in the pirate world.' You were like, 'There's a robot named SM-33.' I said, 'Oh, 'Smee', that's very ''Peter Pan''.' And then: 'Jude Law is going to star.' I was like, 'Oh! I think I just made this?' To do it again in ''Star Wars'' was a dream come true.|David Lowery|<ref name="EmpireOnline">{{WebCite|url=https://www.empireonline.com/tv/news/star-wars-how-jon-watts-assembled-skeleton-crew-directors-exclusive|text=Star Wars: How Jon Watts Assembled His Skeleton Crew Director Team, From Daniels To David Lowery|author=Ben Travis|date=2024-10-18|work=[[Wikipedia:Empire (magazine)|Empire]]|archivedate=20241112200937}}</ref>}}
SM-33 first appeared simultaneously in "[[This Could Be a Real Adventure]]"<ref name="Adventure">{{SkeletonCrew|This Could Be a Real Adventure}}</ref> and "[[Way, Way Out Past the Barrier]]"<ref name="Barrier" /> the first<ref name="AdventureEG">{{SW|url=series/skeleton-crew/season-1-episode-1-episode-guide|text="This Could Be a Real Adventure" Episode Guide &#124; Star Wars: Skeleton Crew}}</ref> and second episodes,<ref name="BarrierEG">{{SW|url=series/skeleton-crew/season-1-episode-2-episode-guide|text="Way, Way Out Past the Barrier" Episode Guide &#124; Star Wars: Skeleton Crew}}</ref> respectively, of the first season of the [[Disney+]] live-action series ''[[Star Wars: Skeleton Crew]]''.<ref name="AdventureEG" /><ref name="BarrierEG" /> The episodes premiered on [[December 2]], [[2024]].<ref name="Release">{{SW|url=news/skeleton-crew-premiere-date|text=''Star Wars: Skeleton Crew'' Will Debut Early|date=2024-11-26}}</ref> SM-33 was first previewed in a teaser trailer<ref name="Europe">{{CelebrationTrailer|''[[Star Wars: Skeleton Crew]]''|Celebration Europe (2023)}}</ref> that was exclusively shown at the Lucasfilm's Studio Showcase during [[Celebration Europe (2023)|Celebration Europe]] on [[April 7]], [[2023]].<ref name="Panel">{{SW|url=news/swce-2023-lucasfilm-studio-showcase|text=SWCE 2023: 11 Highlights from Lucasfilm's Studio Showcase|author=[[Dan Brooks|Broooks, Dan]]|date=2023-04-08}}</ref> [[Jon Watts]]&mdash;creator of ''Skeleton Crew''&mdash;introduced SM-33 and revealed [[Nick Frost]] voiced the droid on [[July 31]], 2024. He shared details on SM-33's role in the story and described Frost's performance as "fun" and "absolutely perfect."<ref name="People" /> From [[August 9]] to [[August 11]],<ref name="D23Run">{{WebCite|url=https://swtorstrategies.com/2024/08/d23-2024-panel-schedule-what-star-wars-fans-can-look-forward-to.html|text=D23 2024 Panel Schedule: What Star Wars Fans Can Look Forward To|author=Kamper, Soeren|date=2024-08-02|work=[https://swtorstrategies.com Star Wars Gaming news]|archivedate=20240907102453}}</ref> during [[The Walt Disney Company|Disney's]] 2024 [[Wikipedia:D23_(Disney)|D23]] event, a model of SM-33 was included<ref name="Model">{{SWYouTube|EHCjIek0uaA|D23: The Ultimate Disney Fan Event Recap &#124; Star Wars|date=2024-08-12}}</ref> for a photo opportunity at the Disney+ Pavilion.<ref name="D23 Panels">{{SW|url=news/d23-2024-panel-schedule|text=Plan Ahead for Everything ''Star Wars'' at D23: The Ultimate Disney Fan Event|date=2024-08-02}}</ref> A model of SM-33 was also showcased during the Creatures & Droids with Legacy Effects panel while [[Alan Scott]] discussed details of SM-33's personality and provided insight on the droid's puppetry. Scott revealed there was a secret name for him and that [[Legacy Effects]] built three versions of the droid, with one being a partial suit, another being a full animatronic puppet, and a digital model for the fighting sequences.<ref name="Twitter">{{Twitter|@ForceGhostPod|status/1822745758170566803|Force Ghost Conversations|date=2024-08-11|quote=Details on SM-33 from #SkeletonCrew #D23|archivedate=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/yVJjj}}</ref>

Without Frost on set,<ref name="FanthaTalks">{{YouTube|video=-Dyudsh5HTk|text=Nick Frost Talks Playing "SM-33" In Disney Plus' Star Wars: Skeleton Crew|channel=@FanthaTracksTV|name=Fantha Tracks TV &#124; Daily Star Wars News|date=2024-11-29}}</ref> someone puppeteered SM-33 while another triggered his dialogue, which was recorded as much as possible by Frost beforehand.<ref name="Prerecord">{{WebCite|url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-skeleton-crew-show-jon-watts-christopher-ford-interview|text=Skeleton Crew's Jon Watts & Christopher Ford On Star Wars Timeline Connections And The Show's Mysteries|author=Deckelmeier, Joe; Hermanns, Grant|date=2024-12-02|work=[[Wikipedia:Screen Rant|''Screen Rant'']]|archivedate=20241212221704}}</ref> When new lines were needed, the series's directors took turns doing SM-33 impressions.<ref name="Ready">{{SW|url=news/skeleton-crew-jon-watts-christopher-ford-interview|text=With ''Star Wars: Skeleton Crew'', Jon Watts and Chris Ford are Ready for Their Own Pirate Adventure|author=[[Kristin Baver|Baver, Kristin]]|date=2024-11-01}}</ref> Frost came in to record the new lines about every six months, over a two to three year period. The actor occasionally pitched line changes for SM-33, which the writers were receptive to, which Frost found to be a nice, collaborative process.<ref name="FanthaTalks" /> Watts said the recordings were helpful for the actors to understand the tone and vibe of SM-33. He further remarked that SM-33 injected humor and levity into the perilous adventure.<ref name="Ready" /> [[Rob Ramsdell]] was SM-33's performance artist and [[Zach Andrews]] his stunt double.<ref name="Barrier" />

According to director [[David Lowery]], SM-33 is a reference to [[Wikipedia:Mr. Smee|Smee]] from ''[[Wikipedia:Peter and Wendy|Peter Pan]]''. Lowery previously directed the [[2023]] Disney film ''[[Wikipedia:Peter Pan & Wendy|Peter Pan & Wendy]]'', which also featured [[Jude Law]] as [[Wikipedia:Captain Hook|Captain Hook]].<ref name="EmpireOnline" /> Watts described SM-33 as the worst babysitter.<ref name="Prerecord" /> [[Ryan Kiera Armstrong]], who portrayed Fern, loved interacting with the SM-33 puppet on set.<ref name="Kiera">{{WebCite|url=https://screenrant.com/star-wars-skeleton-crew-cabot-conyers-kratter-smith-armstrong-interview|text=Skeleton Crew's Young Cast Talk Their Star Wars Characters & Working With Jude Law|author=Deckelmeier, Joe; Hermanns, Grant|date=2024-11-28|work=[[Wikipedia:Screen Rant|''Screen Rant'']]|archivedate=20241128205858}}</ref>
Frost originally turned down a role in ''[[Star Wars]]'' because he did not want to see his face,<ref name="Reject">{{Apple|subdomain=podcasts|url=gb/podcast/nick-frost-aka-british-national-treasure/id1522231252?i=1000511444274|text=Nick Frost - aka British national treasure–Celebrity Catch Up: Life After That Thing I Did|date=2021-03-03}}</ref> but he felt that SM-33 was a good compromise as he would only have to look at the droid.<ref name="FanthaTalks" />

The [[Databank]] states that SM-33 is 2.26 [[meter]]s tall.<ref name="Databank" /> However, in the [[Star Wars Insider 232|''Star Wars Insider'' 232]] article "[[Star Wars: Skeleton Crew Companion|''Star Wars: Skeleton Crew'' Companion]]," [[Jude Law]] states that SM-33 is around 6 [[Foot|feet]] 5 inches fully extended,<ref name="Skeleton Crew Companion">{{InsiderCite|232|Star Wars: Skeleton Crew Companion|''Star Wars: Skeleton Crew'' Companion}}</ref> which is only 1.96 meters.

